      idea: String.raw`Nyquist and Hartley had counted the symbols a telegraph line can carry, and engineers fought noise by repetition and more power. Shannon's insight was statistical: a message is one choice among many, English is a highly constrained stochastic process (he showed it with his series of approximations, built by opening a book at random), and its redundancy, roughly half of what we write, is what codes can remove or exploit. He defined entropy (info-1.2) and proved its uniqueness (info-1.9), the equipartition of typical sequences (info-3.1) and the source coding theorem (info-4.5); he defined channel capacity (info-5.2) and proved the noisy-channel coding theorem (info-5.11) with a random-coding argument nobody had imagined, and the capacity of the band-limited Gaussian channel (info-6.8). The paper built on Markov chains (stoch-1.1) and founded information theory: compression, error-correcting codes approaching capacity (turbo and LDPC codes, polar codes), and the bit itself, a word he credited to J. W. Tukey.`,
